The round is complete. Summary of what happened:

**Built and raced this round:** a cross-plane software-pipelined y-pass (`fold31_ypipe`) at L=31 — plane x+1's access-bound U/V fold interleaved between plane x's FMA-bound C-GEMM kernels into a second arena-resident U/V set, bit-identical by construction (cmp-verified at all 14 dev cells and on the node at m=140). It was motivated by my own r11 finding that both machine pools run half-idle (p0+p5 1.44/2.0, 512-bit accesses 0.72/1.12).

**Verdict: rejected on counters.** +7% time (117.5 vs 109.7 us/step, 4/4 interleaved same-core rounds, with a same-source knob arm proving it's not layout luck); port uops identical to four digits but l1d.replacement +33% (1.22G → 1.62G) — the y-pass hot set sits at the 48 KB L1D boundary and cross-plane concurrency thrashes it. A footprint-minimized variant (shared U, V-only ping-pong) recovered a third of the loss (115.5, fills 1.55G), confirming the mechanism monotonically. The shipped default reverts to the serial pass — bit-identical and parity-raced against the r11 control (109.6-110.2 both arms) — with the variants kept behind `-DGDP_YPIPE`/`-DGDP_YPIPE_S` for cross-arch races. All gates pass with the exact inherited digits; MKL same core/window 848.3 us → 7.7x. Strategy record appended with the mechanism, the "cost fusion candidates in concurrent L1-resident bytes" transfer note for the L=100 crews, and the warning not to rediscover this as a zx-block interleave.

Ended on: serial y-pass retained after the pipelined variant lost on L1-capacity counters — **best node (a80n0) numbers: 109.80 us/step batched (B=16, m=140) and 110.39 us/step at B=1; rel L2 3.92e-16 single / 2.55e-14 on the m=140 map-chain (anchor 2.31e-14), two-step gate 1.73e-15.**
